{# ============================================================================
   HYPER · SECTION COLOURS — a per-widget palette override
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Every widget in this theme paints itself from the same design tokens
   (--color-text-primary, --color-accent, --color-border …). So redefining those
   tokens on the section's own root element is all it takes to recolour
   everything inside it — the widget's bespoke CSS *and* the shared primitives
   it borrows (.hy-btn, .hy-price, .hy-label, and the legacy .taqa-* set)
   — without editing a single existing rule.

   ── Contract ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The caller passes exactly two things:

     scope   the section's CSS selector, e.g. '#hy-cols-' ~ id
     c       the widget's whole data object (widget.data)

   Every widget exposes the same field names, so the call is one identical line
   at the end of each widget file:

     ui "section-colors.njk", scope='#hy-xx-' ~ id, c=d

   ⚠️  `scope` MUST be the id the widget actually renders. A stale prefix here
   (the PDP shipped `#taqa-pd-` while rendering `#hy-pdp-`) fails silently: the
   style block is emitted, matches nothing, and every picker looks dead.

   ── The switch ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   `colors_enabled` off → this file emits NOTHING, and the section falls
   straight back to the theme palette. That is the revert: the merchant's
   picked colours stay saved and come back the moment the switch goes on again.
   An individual picker left empty does the same for that one surface, so
   nobody has to clear a field to undo it.

   ── Three things that are load-bearing ─────────────────────────────────────
   1. This MUST be included AFTER the widget's own <style> block. Both target
      the same id with identical specificity, so source order decides.

   2. `--hy-sec-bg` is why the background picker works at all. Several widgets
      paint their root from an INLINE `style="background: …"` (that is how the
      `background` select — white / warm off-white — is applied), and an inline
      declaration beats any stylesheet rule no matter how specific. So those
      widgets write `background: var(--hy-sec-bg, <their default token>)`
      instead, and the override lands on the CUSTOM PROPERTY, which this block
      does win. The plain `background:` below stays for the widgets that paint
      their root from a class rule instead.

   3. The accent tints are derived with color-mix() rather than exposed as more
      pickers. A merchant who picks one brand colour should not then have to
      hand-pick its hover, its wash and its ring — and if they did, nothing
      would stop the four drifting out of relation to each other.
   ========================================================================= #}

{% set c = data.c | default({}) %}
{% set sc = data.scope %}

{% if c.colors_enabled and sc %}
<style>
  {{ sc }} {
    {% if c.color_bg %}
    /* the section's own surface — see note 2 above */
    --hy-sec-bg: {{ c.color_bg }};
    --color-bg-primary: {{ c.color_bg }};
    --color-bg-secondary: {{ c.color_bg }};
    /* the header paints from its own token family */
    --color-header: {{ c.color_bg }};
    /* …and so does the footer. `--fo-bg` is separate from `--color-footer` on
       purpose: the subscribe button paints its LABEL with the footer's ground,
       so it needs the picked colour, not the tone default. */
    --color-footer: {{ c.color_bg }};
    --fo-bg: {{ c.color_bg }};
    {% endif %}

    {% if c.color_card %}
    --color-surface: {{ c.color_card }};
    --color-bg-tertiary: {{ c.color_card }};
    {% endif %}

    {# titles and any strong text #}
    {% if c.color_title %}
    --color-text-primary: {{ c.color_title }};
    --color-header-text: {{ c.color_title }};
    {# Copy laid over a photograph (hero, banner, video, newsletter in their
       `is-light` tone) is white by default and beats the token above on
       specificity. These two let the picker reach it anyway; unset, the CSS
       fallback keeps the white. #}
    --hy-title-on-media: {{ c.color_title }};
    --color-footer-heading: {{ c.color_title }};
    {# Same trick as `--hy-sec-bg` (note 2 above), for TEXT. A few sections
       paint from a bespoke token of their own rather than from
       `--color-text-primary` — the testimonials band sets `--hy-q-fg` from its
       own picker, which meant this override reached nothing there. Those
       widgets now read `var(--hy-sec-title, <their own default>)`, so the pick
       lands on the custom property and wins without touching their defaults. #}
    --hy-sec-title: {{ c.color_title }};
    {% endif %}

    {# body copy and captions ride together — splitting them gives the merchant
       a way to make captions unreadable and no way to notice #}
    {% if c.color_text %}
    --color-text-secondary: {{ c.color_text }};
    --color-text-tertiary: {{ c.color_text }};
    --hy-text-on-media: {{ c.color_text }};
    --color-footer-text: {{ c.color_text }};
    --color-footer-text-muted: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_text }} 72%, transparent);
    {# the secondary half of the bridge described above #}
    --hy-sec-text: {{ c.color_text }};
    {% endif %}

    {% if c.color_accent %}
    --color-accent: {{ c.color_accent }};
    --color-border-focus: {{ c.color_accent }};
    --color-accent-hover: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 88%, #000);
    --color-accent-dark:  color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 72%, #000);
    --color-accent-soft:  color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 38%, #FFF);
    --color-accent-light: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 12%, #FFF);
    {# `--color-ink*` is a second family that predates `--color-accent*` and is
       still what .taqa-btn--primary, the qty stepper and a few insets paint
       from. In the default palette the two are the same colour, so leaving ink
       behind made an accent pick apply to only half a section. #}
    --color-ink: {{ c.color_accent }};
    --color-ink-hover: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 88%, #000);
    --color-ink-light: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 12%, #FFF);
    {% endif %}

    {% if c.color_border %}
    --color-border: {{ c.color_border }};
    --color-border-strong: {{ c.color_border }};
    --color-border-hover: {{ c.color_border }};
    --color-header-border: {{ c.color_border }};
    --color-footer-border: {{ c.color_border }};
    {% endif %}

    {# for the widgets that paint their root from a class rule rather than an
       inline style — `#id` outranks any class, so this one wins there #}
    {% if c.color_bg %}background: {{ c.color_bg }};{% endif %}
  }

  {# The CTA gets its own pair. It is routinely a different colour from the
     accent that tints links, icons and the highlighted word in the title.

     Every button family is listed. `.hy-btn--*` is what most sections render;
     `.taqa-btn--*` is still emitted by the pages that have not been rebuilt
     yet; `.hy-cd-cta` is the countdown banner's bespoke pill, which belongs to
     neither family and so was invisible to both pickers. Dropping either one silently breaks the colour picker on half
     the theme — which is exactly what happened while this partial listed only
     the old names. #}
  {% if c.color_btn_bg %}
  {{ sc }} .hy-btn--solid,
  {{ sc }} .hy-btn--light,
  {{ sc }} .hy-cd-cta,
  {{ sc }} .taqa-btn--primary {
    background: {{ c.color_btn_bg }};
    border-color: {{ c.color_btn_bg }};
  }
  {{ sc }} .hy-btn--solid:hover:not(:disabled),
  {{ sc }} .hy-btn--light:hover:not(:disabled),
  {{ sc }} .hy-cd-cta:hover,
  {{ sc }} .taqa-btn--primary:hover:not(:disabled) {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_btn_bg }} 88%, #000);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_btn_bg }} 88%, #000);
  }
  {{ sc }} .hy-btn--outline,
  {{ sc }} .hy-btn--light-outline,
  {{ sc }} .taqa-btn--outline,
  {{ sc }} .taqa-btn--ghost {
    border-color: {{ c.color_btn_bg }};
    color: {{ c.color_btn_bg }};
  }
  {% endif %}

  {% if c.color_btn_text %}
  {{ sc }} .hy-btn--solid,
  {{ sc }} .hy-btn--solid:hover:not(:disabled),
  {{ sc }} .hy-btn--light,
  {{ sc }} .hy-btn--light:hover:not(:disabled),
  {{ sc }} .hy-cd-cta,
  {{ sc }} .taqa-btn--primary,
  {{ sc }} .taqa-btn--primary:hover:not(:disabled) { color: {{ c.color_btn_text }}; }
  {% endif %}
</style>
{% endif %}
